 "claim": "Call a three-place knot R mutually selecting on cubic systems when (M1) the connected cubic systems on which R fails form a non-empty class recognisable in polynomial time, and (M2) counting R-solutions is #P-hard on general cubic systems, so that the class makes easy what is hard elsewhere. Assuming P != NP, among all 34 three-place knot relations (five classes) exactly the not-all-equal class is mutually selecting: parity fails recognisably but is affine (its counts are polynomial everywhere); the clause never fails on cubic systems (Tovey); the Fibonacci law and x or (y != z) pin a value, so their failures are coNP-complete to recognise; not-all-equal fails exactly on Pfaffian signings (recognisable; checked on every signing with n <= 6: failure iff |det| = #PM) and its failing systems also make the dimer law easy (KNOT-COUPLING-20261031-001). On plain cubic systems the conclusion is unconditional: not-all-equal is the only knot that can fail at all.",
